== Java in General ==
Java has many mature frameworks since it's been leading enterprise
development for 25 years. Java also has three different editions based on use case

Java Editions
----

-> Java SE
--> Standard edition - java.util, java.io, java.net, etc.

-> Jakarta EE (was Java EE)
--> Enterprise Edition - Builds on the SE; adds servlets, Jakarta Persistence API, messaging, etc. - enterprise level

-> Java ME
--> Micro Edition - Subset of SE for embedded/IoT devices. Not used in QA contexts often

SE is most often used for QA

These frameworks include:
----

-> Backend REST/GraphQL API's
--> Spring Boot, Quarkus, Micronaut

-> Microservices
--> Container (docker etc.) friendly JARs, strong logging & debugging tools

-> Enterprise software (ERP, CRM)
--> SAP, Oracle applications run on the JVM

-> Big Data / Data Engineering
--> Apache Hadoop, Spark, Kafka

-> Financial services and Banking
--> Regulated environments value LTS JDK support, strong typing, and auditability

-> Telecommunication services
--> Long lived, mission critical services which require stability rely on Java

== Why Java is useful for QA Engineers ==

Test Frameworks and Tools:
--

-> Junit5
--> Unit test framework

-> TestNG
--> Alternative test framework to Junit with more configuration options

-> REST Assured
--> Java Domain Specific Language (DSL) for testing HTTP API's

-> Selenium
--> Browser Automation Library - UI automation based in Java

-> Maven/Gradle
--> Build and dependency management tools - Run tests and compile JARs, enable CI/CD pipelines

-> Mockito
--> Java mocking framework - Isolate dependencies in unit tests

-> WireMock
--> Http service visualizer

== Key Points ==

-> Python automation scripts call Java APIs at the service/gateway level
-> Integration tests (Junit + REST Assured) test service tier
-> End-To-End tests (Selenium /  Playwright) test the full stack from the client down
-> Contract Tests validate agreements between client and service tiers

== Why Choose Java ==
-> Portability
-> Static typing catches errors before runtime - fewer runtime errors
-> Object Oriented - every method is inside a class
-> JVM is platform neutral (portable)
-> Flexible enterprise services
-> Mature frameworks (Spring Boot)


